          {
            "file": "IMG_9776_aug1_jpg.rf.b76ce0ba41eb4c4c32828f376f9d9a1e.jpg",
            "direction": "Campsis(GT) -> Madame Galen(pred)",
            "models_affected": [
              "SegFormer",
              "YOLO11seg"
            ],
            "note": ""
          },
          {
            "file": "IMG_1514_aug1_jpg.rf.24e5c4c4be74df738d589bd885434d74.jpg",
            "direction": "Campsis(GT) -> Madame Galen(pred)",
            "models_affected": [
              "YOLO11seg"
            ],
            "note": ""
          },
          {
            "file": "IMG_1801_aug1_jpg.rf.74cfe814835d397f0878eb2ad38c1736.jpg",
            "direction": "Madame Galen(GT) -> Campsis(pred)",
            "models_affected": [
              "SegFormer"
            ],
            "note": "Reverse direction -- confirms the confusion is genuinely bidirectional/symmetric, not a one-way bias."
          }
        ],
        "visual_cause_identified": "Madame Galen is taxonomically Campsis x tagliabuana 'Madame Galen' -- a cultivar hybrid of Campsis radicans itself -- so the two share near-identical compound pinnate leaflets, growth habit, and reddish-brown woody stems. Direct inspection of IMG_1144 (the worst, most-repeated confusion case) and two independently-checked, correctly-classified Madame Galen reference images (IMG_1379, TRT_20_MG) shows NONE of them have visible flowers -- all are dense, purely vegetative foliage. The two species/cultivars are primarily distinguishable by flower color when blooming (Campsis radicans: orange-red; Madame Galen: salmon/apricot); with no flowers visible, there is genuinely little to no reliable visual signal to separate them from foliage alone. This is a real botanical/dataset-level ambiguity, not merely a model weakness, and is worth stating as such in the manuscript."
      },
      "other_recurring_confusions": [
        {
          "pair": "Parthenocissus <-> multiple species (Akebia, Hydrangea, Campsis/Lonicera)",
          "example_file": "IMG_9628_aug1_jpg.rf.d5a8f1f87485c6cdc40.jpg",
          "note": "This single image was confused with a DIFFERENT wrong species by nearly every architecture (UNet->Campsis/Lonicera, DeepLabV3->Akebia/Hydrangea, MaskRCNN->Akebia, YOLO11seg->Lonicera). The image itself is visibly dark/low-contrast/hard to distinguish by eye even for a human annotator, suggesting the image quality itself (not a specific species pair confound) is the driver here -- a genuinely ambiguous/hard example rather than a systematic species confusion."
        },
        {
